WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.370
Meet RuVector: memory
for software, at work.

2
00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:12.570
This is RuVector in your browser: a query
walking a graph of three thousand vectors.

3
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.210
It learns from use, keeping what worked,
so the next search does less work.

4
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:30.510
Every engine runs on the same data, so
you can measure the difference yourself.

5
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:43.240
In a supply-chain control tower, the agent with
memory recalls similar past problems before it acts.

6
00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:48.800
Both agents see the same problems. The
one with memory keeps pulling ahead.

7
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:56.050
More exceptions get resolved without
a person, and nothing was retrained.

8
00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:04.280
In a robot fleet, the first robot to find
a blocked aisle shares it with the rest.

9
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.040
Without shared memory, each robot finds
the blockage alone, then stops and waits.

10
00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.030
Same plant, same robots, fewer stops.

11
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:29.140
In a store twin, as a shelf empties, memory
finds notes about this exact problem.

12
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:36.540
Past shift notes are matched by meaning,
not keywords, right in the browser.

13
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:46.530
The manager is off shift, but
what she learned still answers.

14
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:55.710
In a container yard, the twin on the
right remembers when people cross.

15
00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:03.000
Each crossing is recalled by place
and time, so trucks slow down early.

16
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.410
Same trucks, same people,
far fewer close calls.

17
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.440
Out in the field, the machine searches
its memory for where it got stuck before.

18
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.080
So it steers around that soft
ground instead of driving into it.

19
00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:31.680
Same field, same rain. Only the
machine without memory gets stuck.

20
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:43.500
On a pit wall, today's forecast draws a
straight line and misses the tyres fading.

21
00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:49.010
RuVector recalls the closest past
stints and sees the drop-off coming.

22
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.730
When the stint ends, the result goes
back into memory for next time.

23
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:03.400
Every demo here uses synthetic data.
Try RuVector yourself in the Explorer.

